Please do not forgive me for being a bad poet.
My father before me tried writing poetry,
but his lines gave way to the cries of his first child
rhyming with hunger. Do not forgive me for not
writing about a boulevard I have never seen.
About blackbirds on beeches, horse chestnuts, lindens,
plane trees, poplars, cypresses, and old oaks
whose bones became the vessels that ferried 
my ancestors across the sea. I will write 
of how we only name the trees we can eat.
